---
// How the results are made and read (design ch. 8.1): how it runs, who runs
// it, scoring, profiles, adding an implementation, disputing a result,
// asking for a re-run. The one page that explains the vocabulary.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import State from '../components/State.astro'
import type { Group } from '../lib/data'

const REPO = 'https://github.com/happyproto/spaces-conformance'
const states: { group: Group; label?: string; scored: string; text: string }[] = [
  { group: 'pass', scored: 'Passes', text: 'It does what the spec says.' },
  { group: 'deviation', scored: 'Doesn’t pass', text: 'It fails in exactly the way the project told us to expect, and they’re tracking a fix.' },
  { group: 'fail', scored: 'Doesn’t pass', text: 'It fails, and nobody has flagged it yet.' },
  { group: 'fail', label: 'deviation changed', scored: 'Doesn’t pass', text: 'It’s a known gap, but it failed in a different way than expected, so we treat it as a new failure.' },
  { group: 'pass', label: 'fixed, still listed', scored: 'Passes', text: 'It passes now, but the project still lists it as a known gap. Time to take it off the list.' },
  { group: 'provisional', scored: 'Left out', text: 'It fails a check we haven’t yet confirmed on two independent implementations, so the bug could be ours.' },
  { group: 'error', scored: 'Left out', text: 'Something broke on our side. We retry once, and if it breaks again there’s no result.' },
  { group: 'pending', scored: 'Left out', text: 'We can’t test this against this implementation yet. The details say why.' },
  { group: 'untested', scored: 'Left out', text: 'It applies, but we haven’t written the test yet.' },
  { group: 'na', scored: 'Left out', text: 'The implementation doesn’t do the job this check is about.' },
]
---
<Base title="About" description="How we test atproto spaces implementations, how scores work, and how to add a PDS or ask for a re-run." current="about">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About these results</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We test each implementation over HTTP, the same way any app talking to it would. Every check points at the sentence of
      the spec it comes from, so you can always see why something passed or failed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="how">How we test</h2>
    <p>
      For each run we start a fresh, private network with the implementation, a PLC directory and our test runner, and
      nothing else. Nothing in it can reach the internet. The test runner makes its own accounts and spaces, then works through
      every check that applies. We don&rsquo;t grade the setup, so an implementation that creates spaces in an older way
      still gets tested on everything after that.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>When a project ships a new image, we test it. If nothing surprising turns up, we open a pull request to switch to it. If something does, we open an issue in our repository and stay on the current image.</li>
      <li>Every night, we test anything that changed since its last run: a new image, new tests, or a new spec revision. If nothing changed, we don&rsquo;t run it again.</li>
      <li>Every result goes into the <a href={`${REPO}/tree/results`}>results branch</a> as it was recorded, along with the list of known gaps it was judged against. We never edit a result. If one is wrong, a new run replaces it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="who">Who runs this</h2>
    <p>
      We&rsquo;re <a href="https://happyproto.at/">HappyProto</a>. We also make HappyView, and it&rsquo;s tested here like
      everything else: from its published image, by the same tests, with its whole list of known gaps in view.
      Our test runner implements the spec&rsquo;s crypto and wire formats itself, straight from the spec, and doesn&rsquo;t use
      any implementation&rsquo;s code.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scoring">How scores work</h2>
    <p>
      Every check has a level. <strong>Required</strong> checks come from a MUST in the spec, or from a sentence that defines a
      format, field or method. <strong>Recommended</strong> checks come from a SHOULD, and get their own score.{' '}
      <strong>Info</strong> checks just record what an implementation does where the spec doesn&rsquo;t say, and never count.
    </p>
    <p>
      An implementation&rsquo;s score is the share of required checks it passes, for each job it does. Known gaps still count
      as failures, so listing one never raises a score.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="rows-wrap" style="margin-block-start: var(--hp-space-6); max-inline-size: 60rem">
    <table class="rows">
      <thead><tr><th class="col-state">Result</th><th style="inline-size: 8rem">Counts as</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {states.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="col-state"><State group={s.group} label={s.label} /></td>
            <td>{s.scored}</td>
            <td class="why">{s.text}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-block-start: var(--hp-space-8)">
    <h2 id="provisional">Why some failures don&rsquo;t count yet</h2>
    <p>
      Tests that check crypto can have bugs too. So until one of those checks has passed on two implementations that don&rsquo;t
      share code for it, a failure is shown but doesn&rsquo;t count. Two projects built on the same library count as one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="profiles">Jobs and kinds of host</h2>
    <p>
      The spec splits the work into three jobs. A <strong>repo host</strong> stores and serves people&rsquo;s space repos. A{' '}
      <strong>space host</strong> handles credentials, who can write, and notifications. A <strong>PDS</strong> also has to
      serve a baseline set of methods. Each implementation tells us which jobs it does, and only those checks apply.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some rules assume a space belongs to an account someone signs in as. A <strong>standalone space host</strong> keeps its
      spaces on DIDs it creates, so those rules don&rsquo;t apply to it. We decide which checks each kind of host gets, not the
      implementation, so nobody can opt out of a check. Each implementation&rsquo;s page says what doesn&rsquo;t apply to it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="adding">Adding an implementation</h2>
    <p>
      The easiest way is to <a href={`${REPO}/issues/new?template=add-a-pds.yml`}>open an &ldquo;Add a PDS&rdquo; issue</a>,
      and we&rsquo;ll take it from there. If you&rsquo;d rather send a pull request to{' '}
      <a href={REPO}>happyproto/spaces-conformance</a>, add a folder under <code>implementations/</code> with:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>a manifest: the jobs it does, what it supports, and which library handles each piece of crypto;</li>
      <li>a compose file that starts it on our test network;</li>
      <li>an adapter for creating accounts and spaces, if <code>createAccount</code> and <code>createSpace</code> aren&rsquo;t enough;</li>
      <li>its image, pinned by digest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Its list of known gaps can live in our repository or in its own.</p>

    <h2 id="disputing">If a result looks wrong</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={`${REPO}/issues/new`}>Open an issue</a> with the run id from the implementation&rsquo;s page and the name of the
      check. We assume our tests are wrong until proven otherwise. If they are, we fix them, and the next run replaces the
      result. We never post on another project&rsquo;s tracker automatically.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rerun">Asking for a re-run</h2>
    <p>
      We only test something again when it changes. If you think a result was a fluke, fill in the{' '}
      <a href={`${REPO}/issues/new?template=rerun.yml`}>re-run request</a> with the implementation, the run id from its page,
      and why. One of us will start a fresh run.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
